To match lighting between live-action footage and digital elements, artists use a combination of on-set data capture and digital recreation techniques:

•	On-Set Reference: During filming, the production team often captures "HDR light probes" (chrome or grey spheres) and 360-degree photography of the set (0:36 - 0:50). This provides an accurate 3D map of the light intensity, color, and direction for any given shot.
•	Digital Lighting Setup: Using this captured data, digital artists recreate the environment's lighting within their 3D software (0:54 - 1:17). They place virtual light sources to mimic the real-world setup, ensuring that shadows cast by digital objects align perfectly with shadows in the live-action footage.
•	Compositing Adjustments: In the final compositing phase, artists perform subtle color grading and light wrapping. This blends the edges of the digital assets with the plate footage, ensuring the highlights, reflections, and overall atmosphere feel consistent across the entire composite (1:08 - 1:17).
Compositing blends multiple images by layering them to create a single, cohesive final shot. In the context of The Miniature Wife (0:36 - 1:17), the process involves several key technical steps:

•	Matting and Isolation: Artists first use greenscreen photography to isolate subjects. They create a "matte" or alpha channel, which defines which parts of the image are opaque (the actor) and which are transparent (the green background).
•	Layering: Once isolated, the subject is placed over a background plate (a different, full-scale environment). Compositors adjust the scale and perspective of the subject to match the 12:1 ratio required for the miniature effect.
•	Integration: To make the blend look realistic, artists perform:
•	Color Correction: Matching the color, contrast, and brightness of the foreground subject to the background plate.
•	Lighting Matching: Adding digital light wraps, reflections, and shadows to ensure the subject appears to belong in the new environment.
•	Grain and Blur Matching: Applying the same film grain or motion blur across all layers so they share the same physical texture.

